1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,365
A render farm is the most expensive machine in a studio,

2
00:00:04,468 --> 00:00:07,470
and nobody watches it in real time.

3
00:00:07,588 --> 00:00:12,669
When a job hangs overnight on eight GPUs, the cost isn't the compute.

4
00:00:12,772 --> 00:00:18,235
It's the three shots that miss the 9am review — three recoverable by freeing slots,

5
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:22,868
forty-two GPU slots held by zombie and idle-queue jobs.

6
00:00:22,972 --> 00:00:28,908
This is 243.8 million rows of frame-level telemetry in ClickHouse.

7
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:35,601
198 thousand jobs, 240 hosts, and it's still being written to while we watch.

8
00:00:35,716 --> 00:00:39,613
Three Gemini agents on Google Cloud ADK.

9
00:00:39,724 --> 00:00:44,384
The Sentinel isn't given queries — it's given the schema and a goal,

10
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:49,384
and it writes its own SQL through the official ClickHouse MCP server.

11
00:00:49,492 --> 00:00:55,086
You're watching it compose those against 243.8 million rows, live.

12
00:00:55,204 --> 00:00:57,798
None of them are in our repo.

13
00:00:57,916 --> 00:01:02,168
When it finds an out-of-memory failure it runs an ASOF join

14
00:01:02,284 --> 00:01:05,878
the last telemetry sample before the job died.

15
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:11,153
This one died five seconds after the host hit 97% VRAM.

16
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:16,591
2.2 million rows touched, and the panel tells you exactly what it cost.

17
00:01:16,708 --> 00:01:22,381
Almost nothing else lets you ask a database for the row just before a moment in a single join

18
00:01:22,492 --> 00:01:26,981
anywhere else that's a window function or a correlated subquery.

19
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:32,892
Error text is embedded with Vertex AI and searched by cosine distance in ClickHouse.

20
00:01:33,004 --> 00:01:36,046
It matches meaning, not keywords

21
00:01:36,148 --> 00:01:40,190
so it finds the ticket from March and the fix that closed it.

22
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,434
The Orchestrator weighs findings against the dailies schedule.

23
00:01:44,548 --> 00:01:49,919
A zombie on a show with a review in five hours beats a bigger overrun due tomorrow.

24
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,575
And when a proposal claims it protects a shot,

25
00:01:53,692 --> 00:01:58,155
that claim is checked against the delivery board before the row is written

26
00:01:58,276 --> 00:02:02,844
a shot that's actually on track gets the claim dropped, not recorded.

27
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:06,537
The agent doesn't get to mark its own homework.

28
00:02:06,652 --> 00:02:09,575
Nothing has touched a render host.

29
00:02:09,676 --> 00:02:14,783
A proposal is a record, and the number only moves when a human approves it

30
00:02:14,884 --> 00:02:18,741
because an agent finding waste doesn't reduce your bill.

31
00:02:18,844 --> 00:02:22,044
A supervisor approving the fix does.

32
00:02:22,156 --> 00:02:26,921
Gemini, Google Cloud ADK, ClickHouse through MCP.

33
00:02:27,028 --> 00:02:34,478
This run cost about seven cents and found five thousand nine hundred seventy-three dollars of capacity that's burning right now

34
00:02:34,588 --> 00:02:37,682
and the reviews it was about to cost.
